    {
      "id": "def-cellular-algebra-basis-and-cell-module",
      "provider": "existing current-run native manifest contract",
      "path": "research/frontier-44-special-representation-examples-28-batch-20.pages.json",
      "manifest_sha256": "7024ad09c7d97ea4514d00fbe9f4c6b37a0a3f30aa66a5c44ac607174364d629",
      "contract_sha256": "e38ddbc234941899eea0a278a2008afe0f747ff2136fdd902054c1e1ea984a38",
      "literal_statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with $1$ and $A$ a unital $R$-algebra ([[def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring]]) which is free as an $R$-module on a finite set $C=\\{c^{\\lambda}_{st}:\\lambda\\in\\Lambda,\\ s,t\\in T(\\lambda)\\}$, where $(\\Lambda,\\unrhd)$ is a finite poset ([[def-partial-order]]; its strict order is written $\\rhd$) and each $T(\\lambda)$ is a finite set. For $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda$ let $A^{\\rhd\\lambda}$ be the $R$-submodule of $A$ spanned by all $c^{\\mu}_{uv}$ with $\\mu\\rhd\\lambda$. Then $(C,\\Lambda)$ is a **cellular basis** of $A$, and $A$ is a **cellular algebra**, if: (i) the $R$-linear map $i:A\\to A$ with $i(c^{\\lambda}_{st})=c^{\\lambda}_{ts}$ for all $\\lambda,s,t$ is an algebra anti-automorphism of $A$, i.e. $i(ab)=i(b)i(a)$ and $i(1)=1$ (equivalently $i$ is an isomorphism of $A$ with the opposite algebra; the displayed formula forces $i^2=\\mathrm{id}$); and (ii) for every $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda$, every $t\\in T(\\lambda)$ and every $a\\in A$ there exist $r_v\\in R$ ($v\\in T(\\lambda)$) with $$c^{\\lambda}_{st}\\,a\\equiv\\sum_{v\\in T(\\lambda)}r_v\\,c^{\\lambda}_{sv}\\pmod{A^{\\rhd\\lambda}}.$$\n\n**Cell modules.** For $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda$ let $C^{\\lambda}$ be the free $R$-module with basis $\\{c^{\\lambda}_t:=c^{\\lambda}_{tt}:t\\in T(\\lambda)\\}$; condition (ii) makes $C^{\\lambda}$ a right $A$-module by $c^{\\lambda}_t a:=\\sum_v r_vc^{\\lambda}_v$ when $c^{\\lambda}_{tt}a\\equiv\\sum_v r_vc^{\\lambda}_{tv}\\pmod{A^{\\rhd\\lambda}}$, and it makes $C^{\\lambda}$ a left $A$-module via $i$. The module $C^{\\lambda}$ is the **(right) cell module** of $\\lambda$, and $A^{\\unrhd\\lambda}/A^{\\rhd\\lambda}\\cong C^{\\lambda}\\otimes_R C^{\\lambda}$ via $c^{\\lambda}_{st}+A^{\\rhd\\lambda}\\mapsto c^{\\lambda}_s\\otimes c^{\\lambda}_t$. A cellular algebra may have several cellular bases.",
      "status": "inherited narrow supplier; parent owns its proof and closure, no native acceptance inferred"
    },
